WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.470
Navigation interference threatens more than the map display.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:07.000
It can affect position hold, automated routes, return-to-home, geofencing,

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.075
camera targeting, mapping overlap, flight logs, timestamps,

00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:12.870
and the crew's shared understanding of where the aircraft is.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.025
Automated flight can continue using incorrect assumptions.

00:00:16.025 --> 00:00:18.530
A waypoint route may diverge, pause, or terminate.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:22.540
The pilot may spend critical time interpreting warnings while battery, distance, wind,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.116
and obstacles continue to change.

00:00:24.116 --> 00:00:25.960
Return-to-home deserves special caution.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.205
It depends on a correct home point and trustworthy current position,

00:00:29.205 --> 00:00:33.070
along with navigation, return altitude, wind, and obstacle assumptions.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.285
If those inputs are doubtful, automatic return may create risk rather than solve it.

00:00:37.285 --> 00:00:41.480
Geotagging and map products can become unreliable even when visible imagery remains

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.020
useful.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:46.635
Analysts should not treat location metadata as authoritative after a navigation anomaly.

00:00:46.635 --> 00:00:49.060
Flag affected data for review and corroboration.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.020
Downstream systems may ingest those records automatically.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.980
Notify mapping, inspection, evidence,

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.340
or analytics owners when location or time confidence is reduced so questionable data does

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.840
not silently become an authoritative business record.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.600
Crew confusion can amplify the hazard.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.855
One person may trust the screen while another trusts visual cues.

00:01:05.855 --> 00:01:10.400
Predefined communication and authority help the pilot prioritize aircraft control instead

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.085
of debating attribution in flight.

00:01:12.085 --> 00:01:15.850
Safety priorities are people, other aircraft, property, and controlled recovery.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.800
Mission data and equipment come afterward.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.570
Reduce dependence on automation, preserve orientation and battery margin,

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.620
select a safe landing, and document the anomaly once immediate risk is controlled.
